{"date": "2026-10-23", "post_id": 1499061, "title": "Andromeda in Southern Spring", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-23-andromeda-in-southern-spring/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-january-18-from-orion-to-the-southern-cross/\">Looking toward</a> the north from Mullion, New South Wales, Australia, the Andromeda Galaxy never gets more than a few degrees above the horizon. During the southern hemisphere spring, Andromeda is highest in the sky around midnight though. And in this telephoto scene from October 10/11, multiple exposures were used to reveal the spiral galaxy in tantalizing detail as it climbed above rolling Australian hills. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=220119\">Also known as M31</a>, the Andromeda Galaxy is one of the brightest objects in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-messier-catalog/\">Messier catalog</a>, but is only visible to the unaided eye as a small, faint, fuzzy patch. Digitally tracked and stacked frames <a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_M31_planning_info.jpg\">were combined</a> with a foreground shot in the deep astro-image composite to reveal the galaxy's greater extent in planet Earth's night. In the springtime night skyscape,the closest large spiral galaxy <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=200925\">spans nearly 6 full moons</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>pixels in space<br>", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.astrolucyhu.com/about-lucy\">Lucy Yunxi Hu</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.astrolucyhu.com/about-lucy\">Lucy Yunxi Hu</a>", "alt": "The Andromeda galaxy above rolling hills.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-23-andromeda-in-southern-spring/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_Lucy.jpg?w=3000&h=2004&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 23 - Andromeda in Southern Spring</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-23-andromeda-in-southern-spring/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Looking toward the north from Mullion, New South Wales, Australia, the Andromeda Galaxy never gets more than a few degrees above the horizon. During the southern hemisphere spring, Andromeda is highest in the sky around midnight though. And in this telephoto scene from October 10/11, multiple exposures were used to reveal the spiral galaxy in tantalizing detail as it climbed above rolling Australian hills. Also known as M31, the Andromeda Galaxy is one of the brightest objects in the Messier catalog, but is only visible to the unaided eye as a small, faint, fuzzy patch. Digitally tracked and stacked frames were combined with a foreground shot in the deep astro-image composite to reveal the galaxy&#039;s greater extent in planet Earth&#039;s night. In the springtime night skyscape,the closest large spiral galaxy spans nearly 6 full moons.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: pixels in space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"andromeda in southern spring\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Andromeda in Southern Spring\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-23-andromeda-in-southern-spring/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_Lucy.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=2004&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_Lucy.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=2004&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The Andromeda galaxy above rolling hills.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-23T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-21T12:48:52-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Andromeda in Southern Spring\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_Lucy.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=2004&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 23<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_Lucy.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_Lucy.jpg\" alt=\"The Andromeda galaxy above rolling hills.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Andromeda in Southern Spring </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.astrolucyhu.com/about-lucy\">Lucy Yunxi Hu</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-january-18-from-orion-to-the-southern-cross/\">Looking toward</a> the north from Mullion, New South Wales, Australia, the Andromeda Galaxy never gets more than a few degrees above the horizon. During the southern hemisphere spring, Andromeda is highest in the sky around midnight though. And in this telephoto scene from October 10/11, multiple exposures were used to reveal the spiral galaxy in tantalizing detail as it climbed above rolling Australian hills. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=220119\">Also known as M31</a>, the Andromeda Galaxy is one of the brightest objects in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-messier-catalog/\">Messier catalog</a>, but is only visible to the unaided eye as a small, faint, fuzzy patch. Digitally tracked and stacked frames <a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/APOD_M31_planning_info.jpg\">were combined</a> with a foreground shot in the deep astro-image composite to reveal the galaxy's greater extent in planet Earth's night. In the springtime night skyscape,the closest large spiral galaxy <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=200925\">spans nearly 6 full moons</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>pixels in space<br>\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-22-the-first-green-star-wd-j13306435/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261023/html"}